#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7076c0-fa73-5cb1-9206-2b129dd08be1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f059dab-e348-5008-9ffd-12ad6efc4060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412035} {:answer/id #uuid "867269a7-7e3a-517d-a78e-f1bbdcbc9d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412037} {:answer/id #uuid "a00d9653-e38c-58de-a407-c89baa8a583b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412038} {:answer/id #uuid "c651a004-ab01-5e44-877f-6e709b692934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412043} {:answer/id #uuid "dc097f20-0281-575b-9600-192e1cc05704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00d9653-e38c-58de-a407-c89baa8a583b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412038} :answer-field/id #uuid "87d5341d-a961-523f-bd22-01b7e39702d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764a09e4-f71d-5f0b-9d4b-c619afcb8f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109391" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(-2)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =4:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(4).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(4).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nComputing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-4I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a44a8aa-1d08-5502-a239-743c1cc4e945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412031} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4f7804-44ed-5ae3-aee9-e962469ea61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412032} {:answer/id #uuid "436fb54f-57b5-5820-9d1c-946aae06a6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412033} {:answer/id #uuid "80c047ae-9f8f-55d9-8e22-c0abac0118e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412036} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff8588-b4b4-5b70-8276-5f002a4d8176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a44a8aa-1d08-5502-a239-743c1cc4e945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412031} :answer-field/id #uuid "e32bc50b-3a4b-5de0-9dbe-94f5390440cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c099d5a5-3b8f-5bcf-9f18-6240f88f8abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110376" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-7.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-7)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7076c0-fa73-5cb1-9206-2b129dd08be1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6953f93b-2379-5303-ac2b-efe83a34659b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412034} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b954a7-3c51-5609-9c37-1b99151da2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412039} {:answer/id #uuid "a88be03e-181c-5d7c-ad64-f2d323c34b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412040} {:answer/id #uuid "c41dcc1a-112a-56d9-a7fb-a5379988b9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412042} {:answer/id #uuid "d7499f64-6a9a-55ca-98ee-288f23c9fd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b954a7-3c51-5609-9c37-1b99151da2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412039} :answer-field/id #uuid "35cfa093-b2ba-568a-ad05-ffd2ce87a197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48d72277-36e5-5416-8dca-632269341756" :question/math-academy-id "q-110460" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =7$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =5$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(5)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =7:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(7).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(7).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =7.$\n\nComputing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}]}
